# lazysite users - the FILE FORMAT, for a sysop writing this store by hand.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT COPIED BY THE INSTALLER, and no account here is real.
# Until SM865 the installer seeded `users` from this file, and this file named
# an account called `manager` with no password, in the lazysite-admins group -
# so every fresh install came with a passwordless admin login nobody had asked
# for. A fresh install now has no accounts at all, which is deliberate: make
# the first one with
#
#   lazysite-users.pl --docroot <docroot> setup-sysop --user <name>
#
# which names the person, issues a single-use link for them to set their own
# password, and records who created the account. Prefer it to editing this
# store: it keeps the audit trail naming a person rather than a role.
#
# Format: username:sha256hex(password)
# Generate a hash: echo -n 'password' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1
#
#   alice:5e884898da28047151d0e56f8dc6292773603d0d6aabbdd62a11ef721d1542d8
#
# AN EMPTY PASSWORD FIELD means the account has no password set. Such an
# account can sign in ONLY from localhost (127.0.0.1, ::1) and is refused
# everywhere else with the same answer a wrong password gets, so it cannot be
# used to discover that the account exists (SM798). It is a local bootstrap
# state, not a way to leave an account open:
#
#   alice:
